recommend check-in (Keaton) -- THE GUARD FOR THIS WAS ALREADY RUNNING, AND IT GATED NOTHING. Your card named one line for me -- the GNU-only xargs flag at pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh:158 -- and pressing on it rather than repairing on my own last word is what found the lap. tools/fixtures/s/shell_dialect_scan.sh is rostered, runs every lap, and was printing `advisory_sed_i_bare=14` in green the whole time. ALL FOURTEEN WERE MINE, in the two Pond fixtures whose guards red on the Mac, and the file's own note records FIVE sites measured four days earlier -- so the family grew by nine inside the one lane it breaks, while the number sat in plain sight. ITS PROMOTION CONDITION WAS ALSO ALREADY WRITTEN: the witness header says a family earns its gate by costing a real reading on a real host. Both had cost one. That sentence is the launcher's uid warning again, one layer up -- a note somebody hopes is read, where a ceiling is a thing a program checks. Both are gated now: in-place at ZERO, and readlink's resolve flag a RATCHET AT SEVEN rather than a gate, because five of its sites are the helper's own probe-guarded control comparing against the elder spelling and a sixth is agent-jail.sh, which YOUR accrete-only boundary rightly holds. I declined the exemption that would have made that number look like two. THE SHARPEST FAULT WAS NOT THE FLAG: two plants used `\n` in a replacement, which BSD sed reads as the letter n, so on that bench they would have planted the WRONG TEXT rather than refusing -- a vacuous pass, which is the exact failure this family was booked for. And the named line carried a second fault I could prove here without a Mac: xargs word-splits, so a persist path with a space came back as two lines where one was meant (latent -- it is the fallback leg). WHAT I CANNOT SETTLE, said plainly: whether this turns the Mac's two Pond rows green is its next run's answer, not mine, and the row names that as its falsifier. YOURS, UNCHANGED: rule_twin 39/36 is gate %7; width_check is another body's. MINE NEXT: `env`, the second door duty.
